Key points from this Discussion:

dissociate oculomotor execution (main-sequence kinematics, an index of saccade-generator integrity) from oculomotor policy (when and where gaze is deployed), and characterize how policy parameters, and the magnitude and temporal structure of their variability, change across development


Capacity
Use / policy
Eye
main sequence
saccade rate, timing, targets
Head/body
max sprint, head velocity range (size-normalized)
voluntary speed, turn statistics
Eye–head
passive VOR/OKR gain and phase
anticipatory gaze shifts, stabilization during self-motion

Candidate central claim
Oculomotor execution matures early; eye–head coordination and gaze policy mature later, alongside locomotion.


Locomotion: 

	•	Separate capacity from use, as with the main sequence. Elicit maximal speed (looming or escape, chase) and compare it to voluntary exploratory speed at each age.
	◦	Max sprint low → capacity-limited (muscle, neuromechanics).
	◦	Max sprint high but voluntary speed low → a policy choice. One interesting possibility: animals self-limit speed until they can stabilize gaze at that speed.
	•	Condition on demand. Compare oculomotor policy within matched bins of locomotor speed and head velocity across ages, or fit age × head-kinematics in a mixed model. If the age effect survives matching, it isn’t secondary to the body.

Possible connecting question -> does gaze-stabilization maturity gate running speed, or does running drive gaze maturation




Analysis ideas:

Gaze-shift timing and amplitude. Measure saccade onset relative to head-velocity onset and peak. Also compare saccade amplitude vs. remaining head displacement: a mature system lands gaze where the head will end up

Check VOR (horizontal, vertical) during stationary and running?

measure the eye → head → body onset order and lead times per age from Turn-triggered averages from body trajectory

Predictive horizon (Anticipation). Ask how far ahead eye position or saccade direction predicts body heading change. Use lagged regression (?) between eye, head, and body yaw. A horizon that lengthens with age is a clean anticipation measure.

Saccade end-position eccentricity (Anticipation) . Reset fast phases return the eye toward center; anticipatory ones carry it past center into the turn. Plot end-position vs. head velocity; the slope shift with age indexes anticipation.

Control for head predictability? (Anticipation). Fit an autoregressive model (?) of head velocity per age. If turns simply become more stereotyped, anticipation gets easier without any change in the eye controller. You need to separate those two explanations. 

Staircase index (Active vision during movement). During a turn, measure the fraction of gaze displacement that occurs during saccades vs. between them. A mature saccade-and-fixate pattern approaches 1; smooth drift lowers it. It is a single scalar per turn.


Movement compensation (stabilization). Running produces head pitch/roll oscillations. Can we consider total angular head rotation and locomotion/turning? Is head stabilization increasing with development?
Head rotation vs. locomotion
	•	Smooth running speed (about 100–200 ms window) and align it to the IMU.
	•	Bin speed (e.g., stationary / walk / run). If animals grow a lot, use body lengths/s.
	•	Per bin, compute RMS angular velocity per axis (yaw, pitch, roll), or the gyroscope norm |ω|.
	•	Plot against speed, one curve per age.
	•	Question answered: does a given speed produce less head rotation as animals mature?

VOR gain vs. locomotion
	•	Use inter-saccadic samples only, as in Meyer: eye velocity vs. head velocity, gain = −slope.
	•	Fit speed as a continuous modulator rather than per bin: eye_vel = −(g₀ + g₁·speed)·head_vel + ε
	◦	g₀ is gain at rest.
	◦	g₁ is how locomotion changes gain.
	•	Pair the axes: yaw → horizontal version, pitch → vertical version.
	•	Across animals, use a mixed model with an age × speed interaction (animal as a random effect).
	•	Questions answered: does running change compensation, and does that dependence change with age? In humans, gaze-stabilization strategy shifts with locomotor speed (Dietrich & Wuehr 2019), so a nonzero g₁ is plausible.


Other ideas: 

Eye vs. head contribution to gaze shifts
	•	Gaze shifts averaged about 15.8° from the eye alone, 9.4° from the head alone, and 23.3° combined. The eye contributed more in 83% of saccades. Head-fixed saccades were smaller, about 12.3°
	•	Mice made 1–2 gaze shifts per second during exploration
	•	Dev: track the head/gaze ratio per shift and the gaze-shift rate vs. age. This is where locomotor development should show up: if head capacity grows, the head’s share should grow.

Saccade-triggered profiles and the three-phase structure
	•	Saccade-aligned averages showed slower counter-movements of the eye just before and just after each saccade in freely moving mice; head-fixed mice showed none.
	•	The phases: eye counter-rotation as the head starts moving, then the saccade, then compensation roughly equal and opposite to the head. The pattern held across ~44,000 gaze shifts, implying the head drives gaze shifts.
	•	Asymmetry: temporal-to-nasal saccades were larger. Nasal-to-temporal saccades brought the eye back to center; temporal-to-nasal ones carried it past center in the direction of the turn.
	•	Dev: measure the latency from head-velocity onset to saccade trigger, the amplitude and duration of the pre-saccadic counter-rotation, and the asymmetry vs. age. Also test whether the three-phase pattern is present from the earliest ages or assembles over development.





